<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Apracharajas</h1><p class="st-meta">60 BCE – 60 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A local dynasty of Bajaur in the hills of Gandhara, known from its Buddhist reliquary inscriptions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Apracharajas, kings of the Apracas, ruled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> region north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> from the mid-1st century BCE. They appear to have begun as local chiefs who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a>. The founder known from inscriptions is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayamitra">Vijayamitra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty is known almost entirely from inscriptions in Kharoshthi on Buddhist relic containers, which record the dedication of relics by kings, queens and generals. The reliquaries use several eras, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes</a> era and an era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and are important evidence for the chronology of the period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspavarma">Aspavarma</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indravarma">Indravarma</a>, issued coins.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Apracharajas disappear from the record in the 1st century, probably absorbed by the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. Their reliquaries, now in museums, are among the best sources for early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhara-art">Gandharan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/apracharajas.jpg" alt="Apracharajas" width="320" height="157"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gondophares-Sases_Circa_AD_20-46_Sases_on_horseback.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>60 BCE – 60 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vijayamitra, Indravarma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Apraca kings, Kingdom of Bajaur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=apracharajas" aria-label="Apracharajas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6495 922 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Apracharajas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6815 1178L6948 1391M6815 1178L6757 1168"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6948 1391h0M6757 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6815 1178h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6815 1178h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=0&amp;focus=apracharajas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

